<h1>Monorail &amp; Power-and-Free Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Monorail and power-and-free conveyors use overhead track-mounted carriers to transport products through manufacturing, painting, coating, curing, and assembly processes. A monorail conveyor connects all carriers to a single continuously moving chain, every carrier travels at the same constant speed. A power-and-free conveyor separates the power chain from the product carriers, allowing individual carriers to stop, accumulate, divert to spur tracks, and re-engage with the chain independently. These <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/overhead/">overhead chain conveyor</a> systems free floor space for workers and equipment while routing products through enclosed process environments like paint booths, wash stations, and curing ovens.</p>
<h2 id="comparison-heading">Power-and-Free vs. Single-Chain Monorail</h2>
<p>The feature that separates power-and-free trolleys from monorail trolleys is the connection to the chain: monorail trolleys are fixed to the chain, while power-and-free trolleys ride on a separate free track and are pushed by dogs on the chain, so each load can stop while the chain keeps running. <a href="https://www.pacline.com/overhead-conveyors-101/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PACLINE</a> calls monorail "synchronous" conveying, where all loads move at the same pace, and power-and-free "asynchronous," where each trolley set can stop and start independently. The main advantage of a monorail is simplicity: one track, one chain, and no switches or stops to control.</p>
